# why do we need different range of VPC's for VPC peering in AWS?
1. AWS explicitly blocks overlapping CIDRs.
You cannot create a VPC peering connection if CIDR blocks overlap.

2. AWS cannot "fix" overlapping ranges by translating IPs.

3. Routing must be unambiguous
Each VPC uses CIDR blocks to route traffic internally.
The router cannot determine which VPC should receive traffic for a given IP.
This creates routing ambiguity and breaks connectivity.

✅ ✅ Non-Overlapping CIDR (Allowed ✅
Example 1 (No IP range is shared)
VPC A: 10.0.0.0/16 → (10.0.0.0 – 10.0.255.255)
VPC B: 10.1.0.0/16 → (10.1.0.0 – 10.1.255.255)

Example 2 ( Completely separate networks)
VPC A: 192.168.1.0/24 → (192.168.1.0 – 192.168.1.255)
VPC B: 192.168.2.0/24 → (192.168.2.0 – 192.168.2.255)

Example 3 (Different CIDR sizes)
VPC A: 172.16.0.0/16 → (172.16.0.0 – 172.16.255.255)
VPC B: 172.17.0.0/20 → (172.17.0.0 – 172.17.15.255)

❌ ❌ Overlapping CIDR (Not Allowed ❌)
Example 1 (Subset overlap)
VPC A: 10.0.0.0/16 → (10.0.0.0 – 10.0.255.255)
VPC B: 10.0.1.0/24 → (10.0.1.0 – 10.0.1.255)
👉 VPC B range is inside VPC A

Example 2 (Exact same range)
VPC A: 192.168.1.0/24
VPC B: 192.168.1.0/24
❌ Conflict → Not allowed

Example 3 (Partial overlap) Routing confusion
VPC A: 172.16.0.0/20 → (172.16.0.0 – 172.16.15.255)
VPC B: 172.16.10.0/24 → (172.16.10.0 – 172.16.10.255)
👉 VPC B falls within VPC A

# “Does any IP from VPC B fall inside VPC A range?”
✅ NO → Non-overlapping → Allowed
❌ YES → Overlapping → Not allowed